{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   Implement Attention-based Vision Token Pruning (`--prune_mode attention` / `--mode attention_prune`) for three VLA model eval scripts: π0.5, OpenVLA, and SmolVLA. The goal is to prove that regardless of importance metric (random, l2norm, or attention), CV (coefficient of variation) can predict pruning tolerance. This is marked P1 urgent. The implementation should:\n   - Add attention-based pruning as a new option alongside existing random/l2norm methods\n   - Use LLM attention weights from first 2 layers as importance scores\n   - Keep top-k vision tokens by importance (true_removal mode)\n   - Be validated with dry-run (2 episodes)\n   - π0.5 and OpenVLA are primary targets; SmolVLA is lower priority\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - **Vision Token Pruning**: Removing low-importance vision tokens before LLM processing to reduce computation\n   - **Attention-based importance scoring**: Using attention weights from early LLM layers to determine which vision tokens are most important\n   - **π0.5 architecture**: PaliGemma (SigLIP vision + Gemma 2B LLM) with prefix-based inference, KV caching, and flow matching for action prediction\n   - **Monkey-patching `embed_prefix`**: π0.5's pruning mechanism replaces the model's embed_prefix method\n   - **Forward hook-based pruning**: OpenVLA/SmolVLA use `register_forward_hook` on projector/connector modules\n   - **GQA (Grouped Query Attention)**: Gemma uses 8 Q heads, 1 KV head; requires KV head expansion for attention computation\n   - **RoPE (Rotary Position Embeddings)**: Different implementations - Gemma uses `apply_rotary_pos_emb(q, k, cos, sin, unsqueeze_dim=1)`, Llama uses `rotate_half`\n   - **2-layer attention probe**: Manually running embeddings